  ## SECTION 4 — CLI CONTRACT

  ### Binary

  - ccr

  ### v1 commands

  1. ccr [prompt]
  2. ccr --print [prompt]
  3. ccr sessions list [--cwd PATH] [--json]
  4. ccr sessions show <session_id> [--json]

  ### v1 flags (run path)

  - -p, --print: headless non-interactive mode
  - --output-format {text,json,stream-json} (default text)
  - --input-format {text,stream-json} (default text)
  - -c, --continue: resume latest session in current cwd
  - -r, --resume <session_id>: resume exact session id
  - --fork-session: on resume/continue, write to new session id with parent reference
  - --session-id <uuid>: force session id for new session
  - --permission-mode {ask,auto,bypass} (default ask)
  - --allowed-tools "<specs>" comma/space separated
  - --disallowed-tools "<specs>" comma/space separated
  - --model <name>
  - --fallback-model <name>
  - --max-turns <n> (default 32 in print mode)
  - --system-prompt <text>
  - --append-system-prompt <text>
  - --cwd <path> (default current working directory)
  - --add-dir <path> repeatable
  - --transcript-dir <path> (default ~/.ccr/sessions)
  - --no-session-persistence
  - --tool-timeout-ms <n> default bash timeout override
  - --version
  - --help

  ### Tool spec grammar (allowed/disallowed)

  - ToolName
  - ToolName(pattern)
  - Bash pattern semantics: shell-command prefix wildcard (git:*, pytest:*)
  - File-tool pattern semantics: normalized path prefix
  - grammar is deterministic and whitespace-insensitive around commas

  ### Precedence and filtering

  - disallowed overrides allowed
  - invalid tool specs are hard CLI errors
  - filtered-out tools are treated as hard boundary denials at permission time

  ### Input modes

  - text:
      - prompt from positional arg if present
      - else read full stdin
      - if neither: usage error
  - stream-json:
      - stdin newline-delimited JSON control messages
      - supported message types in v1:
          - user_message
          - permission_decision
          - interrupt

  ### Stream-json control message schemas (v1)

  - user_message:
      - required: type="user_message", content
      - optional: client_msg_id
  - permission_decision:
      - required: type="permission_decision", tool_call_id, decision
      - required decision enum: allow_once|deny_once|allow_session|deny_session|allow_persistent|deny_persistent
      - optional: comment
  - interrupt:
      - required: type="interrupt"
      - optional: reason
  - unknown control message type is rejected with deterministic parse error (exit 2 before run start, runtime
    system_notice if mid-run)

  ### Output modes

  - text: final assistant text to stdout, notices to stderr
  - json: single terminal JSON object with final message + metadata
  - stream-json: NDJSON runtime events conforming to events.schema.json

  ### Permission channel constraints

  - --permission-mode ask requires an interactive decision channel.
  - interactive decision channel exists only when:
      - --input-format stream-json, or
      - terminal interactive mode (non---print) with explicit prompt handling enabled.
  - if ask is required and no interactive decision channel exists, decision is deterministic deny
    (reason_code=ask_unavailable) and process exits 6 when this blocks required operation.

  ### Exit codes

  - 0 success
  - 2 CLI usage/validation error
  - 3 unrecoverable model/provider failure
  - 4 unrecoverable tool-runtime failure
  - 5 permission denied (required operation blocked)
  - 6 permission required but unavailable in current I/O mode
  - 7 interrupted by user
  - 8 transcript persistence/resume corruption
  - 9 internal invariant failure

  ### Resume / continue behavior

  - --continue: load latest session for current cwd from session index, append new run
  - --resume <id>: load exact session
  - --fork-session: create new session id, set resume_parent_session_id
  - resumed run starts with session_resumed event before new user_message
  - each resumed run gets a new run_id and independent run terminal event

  ### Examples

  1. ccr -p "fix failing tests" --permission-mode auto --output-format text
  2. ccr -p --output-format stream-json --input-format stream-json
  3. ccr --continue -p "apply the same fix to module B"
  4. ccr --resume 01JABC... --fork-session -p "try alternative patch"
  5. ccr -p "run lint and fix" --allowed-tools "Read Edit Write Bash(git:*)"

  ———

  ## SECTION 5 — EVENT MODEL

  ### Common envelope (all runtime events)

  - Required fields:
      - event_id
      - session_id
      - run_id
      - seq
      - ts
      - type
      - turn_index
  - Optional fields:
      - parent_event_id
      - message_id
      - tool_call_id
      - model
      - provider
      - meta
  - Deterministic identity/linking:
      - persisted records must satisfy record_id == event_id
      - persisted parent links must satisfy parent_id == parent_event_id
  - Ordering:
      - seq strictly increasing within session_id
      - parent chain must reference an earlier event in same session_id
      - no cycles

  ### Run-scoped terminality

  - terminal events are run-scoped, not session-scoped
  - each (session_id, run_id) must end with exactly one terminal event:
      - session_completed, or
      - session_failed
  - a session may contain multiple runs across resumes
  - later runs do not alter terminal status of earlier runs

  ### Canonical event types

  | Event type | Required fields | Optional fields | Ordering rules | Persistence |
  |---|---|---|---|---|
  | session_start | cwd, permission_mode, model | fallback_model, system_prompt_hash | first event of a new session run
  | persist |
  | session_resumed | resume_from_session_id | forked, resume_parent_session_id | first event of resumed run | persist |
  | user_message | message_id, content | input_mode, client_msg_id | after run start event | persist |
  | assistant_delta | message_id, delta | token_count | only between user_message and matching assistant_message |
  stream-only (never persisted in v1) |
  | assistant_message | message_id, content | usage | closes delta stream for message_id | persist |
  | tool_call_requested | tool_call_id, tool_name, input | source_message_id, provider | after assistant tool intent |
  persist |
  | tool_permission_required | tool_call_id, request_hash, risk_label, interactive_available | candidate_rule_ids |
  emitted only when provisional decision is ask and interactive channel exists | persist |
  | tool_permission_decided | tool_call_id, decision, reason_code, precedence_rank, request_hash, risk_label,
  decision_source | matched_rule_id, matched_rule_effect, matched_rule_lifetime, rule_mutation, explanation | exactly
  once per tool call before execute or denied result | persist |
  | tool_execution_started | tool_call_id, tool_name | timeout_ms, concurrency_group | only when decision is allow |
  persist |
  | timeout_triggered | scope, timeout_ms | tool_call_id | after corresponding started event | persist |
  | tool_result | tool_call_id, result | error | after execution start or after denied decision | persist |
  | tool_execution_finished | tool_call_id, status | duration_ms, recovered | immediately after tool_result for same
  tool_call_id | persist |
  | retry_scheduled | attempt, delay_ms, reason | retry_after_ms | before delayed provider retry | persist |
  | fallback_model_selected | from_model, to_model, reason | attempt | before first fallback request in run | persist |
  | interrupt_received | source | reason | emitted immediately on interrupt signal | persist |
  | system_notice | code, message | severity, details | anywhere, seq-ordered | persist |
  | session_completed | status | usage_summary | terminal event for run | persist |
  | session_failed | error_code, error_message | retryable, details | terminal event for run | persist |

  ### Deterministic tool lifecycle ordering

  - allow path:
      - tool_call_requested
      - optional tool_permission_required
      - tool_permission_decided(decision=allow)
      - tool_execution_started
      - optional timeout_triggered
      - tool_result(status=success|error|timeout|cancelled)
      - tool_execution_finished(status matches tool_result.status)
  - deny path:
      - tool_call_requested
      - tool_permission_decided(decision=deny)
      - tool_result(status=denied)
      - tool_execution_finished(status=denied)
  - ask-unavailable path:
      - tool_call_requested
      - tool_permission_decided(decision=deny, reason_code=ask_unavailable)
      - tool_result(status=denied)
      - tool_execution_finished(status=denied)

  ### Stream-only vs persisted rules

  - assistant_delta is stream-only in v1 and must never be persisted.
  - all other events in the table are persisted exactly once.
  - persisted events have a deterministic 1:1 mapping to JSONL records.

  ———

  ## SECTION 6 — JSONL TRANSCRIPT SCHEMA

  ### Record schema (one JSON object per line)

  - Required fields:
      - schema_version ("1.0")
      - record_id
      - event_id
      - session_id
      - run_id
      - seq
      - ts
      - type
      - turn_index
      - payload
  - Optional fields:
      - parent_id
      - parent_event_id
      - message_id
      - tool_call_id
      - tool_meta
      - permission_meta
      - error_meta
      - resume_meta

  ### Identity and parent guarantees

  - record_id globally unique within transcript corpus.
  - event_id must equal record_id for every persisted event.
  - parent_event_id must equal parent_id when parent exists.
  - parent_id points to a prior record in same session_id.
  - no cycles allowed.
  - append-only: existing lines never edited.

  ### Canonical event -> JSONL mapping

  - mapping rule:
      - for each persisted runtime event, write exactly one JSONL record with identical envelope fields.
      - stream-only events (assistant_delta) produce zero JSONL records.
  - required per-record payload semantics:
      - payload must contain event-type required fields only plus declared optionals.
      - unknown fields are allowed only when schema minor version permits additive fields.
  - normalization:
      - path fields in payload use POSIX separators.
      - timestamps are RFC3339 UTC.
      - enums use lowercase exact literals.

  ### Required metadata objects

  - tool_meta (required when tool_call_id present):
      - required: tool_call_id, tool_name
      - optional: is_dangerous, timeout_ms, provider, concurrency_group
  - permission_meta (required on permission decision records):
      - required:
          - mode
          - decision
          - reason_code
          - precedence_rank
          - decision_source
          - risk_label
          - request_hash
      - optional:
          - matched_rule_id
          - matched_rule_effect
          - matched_rule_lifetime
          - rule_mutation
  - error_meta:
      - required when present: code, message, retryable
      - optional: stack_hash, details
  - resume_meta:
      - optional keys: resume_parent_session_id, interrupted_turn_open, pending_tool_calls, reconciled_tool_calls

  ### Ordering guarantees

  - single-writer lock per session_id.
  - seq allocation occurs only inside the append critical section.
  - write order equals logical event order.
  - each line ends with exactly one newline.
  - transcript writer performs fsync after each persisted record in v1.
  - if crash occurs during append and final line is malformed JSON, loader truncates only the malformed tail and emits
    system_notice(code=TRANSCRIPT_TAIL_TRUNCATED) in next run.

  ### Persistence rules

  - assistant_delta:
      - stream output only.
      - may be buffered in memory for UI output.
      - never persisted to JSONL in v1.
  - partial assistant output:
      - if run interrupts before assistant_message, buffered deltas are discarded.
      - no synthetic partial assistant record is written.
  - permission ask flow:
      - tool_permission_required persisted only when interaction is possible.
      - if interaction unavailable, engine writes only tool_permission_decided deny path.
  - tool result/finalization:
      - tool_execution_finished must immediately follow tool_result for same tool_call_id in normal flow.
      - recovery may append missing tool_execution_finished with recovered=true.

  ### Interrupted session persistence

  - on interrupt:
      1. persist interrupt_received
      2. persist cancellation outcomes for in-flight tools (tool_result + tool_execution_finished with status=cancelled)
         when possible
      3. persist system_notice(code=TURN_INTERRUPTED) if turn was open
      4. persist run terminal session_failed(error_code=INTERRUPTED)
      5. set resume_meta.interrupted_turn_open=true on terminal record if unresolved state remains
  - no backfill mutation of prior lines is permitted.

  ### Resume reconstruction rules

  1. Load all records for session_id ordered by seq.
  2. Validate strict monotonic seq, identity equality (record_id==event_id), and parent chain equality
     (parent_id==parent_event_id).
  3. Partition records by run_id; identify last run terminal event.
  4. Reconstruct session-scoped mutable state by replay:
      - session rules from permission_meta.rule_mutation
      - unresolved tool calls
      - last stable completed turn
  5. Reconcile open tool calls:
      - if tool_execution_started exists without matching tool_execution_finished, append reconciliation records in new
        run marking cancellation.
  6. Start new run with fresh run_id, append session_resumed, then accept new user_message.

  ### Schema versioning and forward compatibility

  - semantic versioning: major.minor.
  - major change: breaking reader contract.
  - minor change: additive optional fields/events only.
  - v1 reader requirements:
      - must read all 1.x.
      - must ignore unknown fields.
      - must reject unknown required fields for known event type.
      - must fail fast on 2.x.

  ### Persistent rules durability

  - persistent permission rules path:
      - ${CCR_CONFIG_DIR}/permissions/rules.v1.json
      - default ~/.config/ccr/permissions/rules.v1.json
  - write strategy:
      - write temp file
      - fsync temp file
      - atomic rename
      - fsync parent directory
  - load failures:
      - engine boots with empty persistent rules
      - emits system_notice(code=PERMISSION_RULES_LOAD_FAILED) once per run

  ———

  ## SECTION 7 — PERMISSION MODEL

  ### Deterministic policy engine contract

  - permission decisions are deterministic and side-effect free until finalize.
  - v1 permission decisions use no ML inference.
  - risk labels are deterministic static labels from tool/type/input facts.
  - decision input tuple:
      - normalized PermissionRequest
      - active rules snapshot
      - permission mode
      - hard-boundary config
      - interactive-channel availability
  - decision output:
      - allow|deny|ask plus structured explanation payload.

  ### Modes

  - ask: unresolved requests require explicit user decision.
  - auto: safe read/metadata requests auto-allow; dangerous/unknown requests require ask.
  - bypass: mode default allow, but hard boundaries still deny.

  ### Strict decision precedence (first match wins)

  | Rank | Condition | Decision | reason_code |
  |---|---|---|---|
  | 1 | hard boundary violation | deny | hard_boundary_* |
  | 2 | matching deny rule | deny | rule_deny |
  | 3 | matching allow rule | allow | rule_allow |
  | 4 | mode is bypass | allow | mode_bypass |
  | 5 | mode is ask and interactive available | ask | mode_ask |
  | 6 | mode is ask and interactive unavailable | deny | ask_unavailable |
  | 7 | mode is auto and risk label is safe_read or safe_metadata | allow | auto_safe |
  | 8 | mode is auto and risk label is dangerous_write, dangerous_exec, or unknown, interactive available | ask |
  auto_requires_user |
  | 9 | mode is auto and risk label is dangerous_write, dangerous_exec, or unknown, interactive unavailable | deny |
  ask_unavailable |
  | 10 | engine internal failure | deny | engine_error |

  ### Rule object schema

  - required:
      - rule_id
      - effect (allow|deny)
      - lifetime (once|session|persistent)
      - source (cli|user|system)
      - match
      - created_seq
      - enabled
  - optional:
      - created_at
      - expires_at
      - remaining_uses (required when lifetime is once)
      - comment
      - modes (default all modes)

  ### Match grammar (v1)

  - matching is exact/prefix only; regex is disallowed.
  - allowed match keys:
      - tool_name
      - request_hash
      - path_exact
      - path_prefix
      - command_exact
      - command_prefix
      - provider
  - normalization before match:
      - canonical absolute path, symlink-resolved
      - command whitespace-collapsed, trailing shell comments removed
  - specificity score:
      - request_hash: 100
      - command_exact or path_exact: 80
      - command_prefix or path_prefix: 60
      - tool_name only: 20

  ### Conflict resolution

  - any matched deny beats any matched allow.
  - within same effect, deterministic winner sort:
      1. source priority: cli, user(session/once), user(persistent), system
      2. specificity score descending
      3. created_seq descending
      4. rule_id ascending
  - no implicit “last prompt wins” behavior exists.

  ### Hard-boundary behavior

  - hard boundaries are non-overridable by rules, mode, or user prompt outcome.
  - required hard-boundary deny codes:
      - hard_boundary_path_outside_root
      - hard_boundary_symlink_escape
      - hard_boundary_tool_disallowed
      - hard_boundary_command_forbidden
      - hard_boundary_network_disabled

  ### Decision semantics for allow/deny/session/persistent

  - allow_once|deny_once:
      - create lifetime=once rule with exact request_hash.
      - rule is consumed only if it is the winning rule.
  - allow_session|deny_session:
      - create lifetime=session rule active for session_id.
      - persisted in transcript via rule_mutation and reconstructed on resume.
  - allow_persistent|deny_persistent:
      - create lifetime=persistent rule persisted to rules file.
  - effect and lifetime are orthogonal dimensions.

  ### Risk labels (deterministic v1)

  - safe_read:
      - Read, LS, Glob, Grep with in-boundary paths
  - safe_metadata:
      - read-only tool metadata queries
  - dangerous_write:
      - Edit, Write, any mutating file tool
  - dangerous_exec:
      - Bash, external process execution
  - unknown:
      - tools not classified by static table

  ### Evaluation flow (exact)

  1. receive PermissionRequest.
  2. normalize request fields and compute request_hash.
  3. evaluate hard boundaries.
  4. load immutable active rules snapshot.
  5. select matching rules.
  6. resolve deny/allow winner with deterministic tie-break.
  7. apply precedence table fallback when no winning rule.
  8. if outcome is ask, emit tool_permission_required and wait for input decision.
  9. map user decision into final allow/deny and optional rule_mutation.
  10. emit tool_permission_decided with full explanation.
  11. append permission audit record.
  12. persist rules mutation when lifetime is persistent.

  ### Rule loading order

  1. built-in hard-boundary configuration
  2. persistent rules file (rules.v1.json)
  3. CLI-derived rules (--disallowed-tools, then --allowed-tools)
  4. session replay mutations from transcript on resume
  5. runtime user mutations

  ### Runtime query interface

  - PermissionRequest required fields:
      - session_id, run_id, turn_id, tool_call_id
      - mode
      - tool_name, provider
      - is_read_only, is_dangerous
      - normalized_path (nullable)
      - normalized_command (nullable)
      - interactive_available
  - PermissionDecision required fields:
      - decision
      - reason_code
      - precedence_rank
      - decision_source
      - risk_label
      - request_hash
      - matched_rule_id (nullable)
      - matched_rule_effect (nullable)
      - matched_rule_lifetime (nullable)
      - explanation

  ### Decision explanation payload

  - required:
      - decision
      - reason_code
      - precedence_rank
      - decision_source
      - risk_label
      - request_hash
      - facts
      - message
  - optional:
      - matched_rule_id
      - matched_rule_effect
      - matched_rule_lifetime

  ### Audit logging requirements

  - append one record per finalized permission decision to:
      - ${CCR_DATA_DIR}/audit/permission_audit.v1.jsonl
  - required fields:
      - schema_version
      - ts
      - session_id
      - run_id
      - turn_id
      - tool_call_id
      - mode
      - tool_name
      - provider
      - request_hash
      - decision
      - reason_code
      - precedence_rank
      - risk_label
      - matched_rule_id (nullable)
      - hard_boundary_code (nullable)
      - engine_version
  - fail-closed rule:
      - if audit append fails, permission outcome becomes deterministic deny with reason_code=audit_write_failed.

  ### Dangerous tool behavior in auto mode

  - Bash, Edit, Write, and any is_dangerous=true tool are never auto-allowed without explicit allow rule.
  - unresolved dangerous request in non-interactive context denies with ask_unavailable.

  ———

  ## SECTION 8 — TOOL CONTRACT AND RESULT ENVELOPE

  ### Base tool contract (v1)

  - name: str
  - version: str
  - input_schema: JSON Schema
  - is_read_only: bool
  - is_dangerous: bool
  - interrupt_behavior: "cancel" | "block"
  - concurrency_group: str (e.g., fs-read, fs-write, shell)
  - validate(input) -> normalized_input
  - execute(context, normalized_input, cancel_token) -> ToolResultEnvelope

  ### Contract invariants

  - input must be schema-valid before execution.
  - output must always be envelope-valid.
  - every call returns exactly one terminal envelope.
  - tools do not read raw CLI flags directly.

  ### Tool must never do

  - bypass permission decisions.
  - write transcript directly.
  - swallow cancellation without reporting cancelled=true.
  - return unstructured exceptions.

  ### Result envelope schema (exact fields)

  | Field | Type | Required |
  |---|---|---|
  | tool_call_id | string | yes |
  | tool_name | string | yes |
  | status | success|error|cancelled|timeout|denied | yes |
  | started_at | RFC3339 | yes |
  | finished_at | RFC3339 | yes |
  | duration_ms | int | yes |
  | stdout | string | no |
  | stderr | string | no |
  | content_blocks | array | yes |
  | diff | string (unified diff) | no |
  | files_read | array[path] | no |
  | files_written | array[path] | no |
  | file_metadata | object | no |
  | error | object {code,message,retryable,details} | no |
  | cancelled | bool | yes |
  | timed_out | bool | yes |
  | permission | object {mode,decision,reason_code,precedence_rank,decision_source,request_hash,matched_rule_id} | yes |
  | sandbox | object {lite_enforced,violations} | yes |

  ### Concurrency expectations

  - read-only tools in fs-read may run concurrently.
  - fs-write tools are serialized per canonical path lock.
  - shell runs one foreground command per session in v1.

  ### Interruption expectations

  - cancel tools terminate promptly on cancel token.
  - block tools complete current atomic step before returning.
  - all interrupted tools emit terminal envelope with status=cancelled.

  ———

  ## SECTION 9 — LOCAL TOOL SPEC

  | Tool | Purpose | Allowed inputs | Safety limits | Failure modes | Permission requirements | Transcript
  representation | Determinism in tests |
  |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
  | Read | file content read | path, optional offset, limit | max bytes 256KB, max lines 5000, path must be allowed |
  not found, binary disallowed, out-of-scope path | read permission; auto-allow in workspace | tool_call_requested +
  tool_result + tool_execution_finished | stable truncation rules and byte counts |
  | LS | list dir entries | path, optional recursive, max_depth | max entries 2000 | not dir, denied path | read
  permission | result includes sorted entries + file type stats | lexicographically sorted output |
  | Glob | match file patterns | pattern, optional base_path | max matches 2000, no parent-escape | invalid pattern,
  denied path | read permission | result includes sorted matched paths | path order stable |
  | Grep | search text content | pattern, path, optional flags (ignore_case, literal) | max matches 2000, per-file cap
  1MB scanned | regex invalid, binary skipped, denied path | read permission | result includes path+line+column+snippet
  | ordered by path then line then column |
  | Bash | execute shell command | command, optional timeout_ms, cwd | default timeout 120000 ms, max 600000 ms, stdout/
  stderr each truncated 256KB | non-zero exit, timeout, spawn failure, cancellation | dangerous; evaluated by
  deterministic command rules + mode | result includes exit code, stdout/stderr, timeout/cancel flags | deterministic
  timeout and truncation |
  | Edit | in-file string replacement with patch | path, old_string, new_string, optional replace_all | file <= 1MB,
  exact match required unless replace_all=true | 0 matches, >1 match with replace_all=false, stale file, denied path |
  write permission + path rules | result includes unified diff + write metadata | deterministic patch format |
  | Write | full file overwrite | path, content, optional if_unmodified_since token | file <= 1MB, atomic write
  temp+rename | stale token mismatch, denied path, write failure | write permission + path rules | result includes bytes
  written + hash + metadata | deterministic hashing and line endings policy |

  ### Determinism policy

  - newline normalization:
      - Edit preserves existing newline style
      - Write uses \n unless preserve_newline_style=true
  - all path outputs normalized to POSIX-style in transcript payloads
  - duration assertions use bounded ranges, not exact ms

  ———

  ## SECTION 10 — RETRY / FALLBACK / INTERRUPT / TIMEOUT

  ### Transient API retry policy

  - retryable classes:
      - HTTP: 408, 409(lock timeout), 429, 500, 502, 503, 504, 529
      - network: connection reset, EOF, DNS temporary failure, socket timeout
  - max attempts per primary model: 5 (attempt 1 + 4 retries)
  - backoff: exponential with jitter
      - base 0.5s, multiplier 2.0, cap 8s, jitter ±20%
  - emit retry_scheduled before each delayed retry

  ### Retry-After handling

  - if Retry-After <= 20s: sleep exact value
  - if Retry-After > 20s and fallback model exists: trigger fallback immediately
  - if Retry-After > 20s and no fallback: wait 20s and continue bounded retries

  ### Fallback policy

  - fallback activation conditions:
      - overload/rate-limit class (429/529) after first failed primary attempt
      - fallback model configured and different from primary
  - emit fallback_model_selected once per run
  - fallback retry budget: max 3 attempts
  - total attempts across primary+fallback: max 8
  - if exhausted: emit run terminal session_failed(error_code=PROVIDER_RETRY_EXHAUSTED)

  ### Subprocess timeout behavior

  - per Bash call timeout source:
      - tool input timeout_ms else session default (120000)
  - on timeout:
      1. emit timeout_triggered(scope=tool, tool_call_id=...)
      2. send SIGTERM
      3. wait 2 seconds
      4. send SIGKILL
      5. emit tool_result(status=timeout)
      6. emit tool_execution_finished(status=timeout)

  ### Interrupt propagation

  - interrupt sources:
      - SIGINT
      - stream-json interrupt message
  - propagation order:
      1. emit interrupt_received
      2. cancel model stream token
      3. cancel cancellable tool executions
      4. emit cancellation tool_result and tool_execution_finished for in-flight calls when possible
      5. emit system_notice(code=TURN_INTERRUPTED) if turn incomplete
      6. emit run terminal session_failed(error_code=INTERRUPTED)

  ### Cancellation semantics

  - model cancel:
      - immediate stop
      - partial assistant_delta is not promoted to assistant_message
  - tool cancel:
      - cancel behavior tools terminate
      - block behavior tools finish current atomic step then terminate
  - interrupted run remains resumable unless transcript write fails irrecoverably

  ### Session recovery after partial failure

  - if provider/tool fails after at least one persisted user turn:
      - emit system_notice and run terminal session_failed
      - keep transcript resumable
  - --resume begins a new run from last committed stable boundary
  - previous run terminality is immutable

  ———

  ## SECTION 11 — SANDBOX STRATEGY

  ### v1 lite sandbox definition

  - enforced in-process policy, not container isolation
  - enforced constraints:
      - canonical path boundary checks on all file tools
      - deny writes outside {cwd + --add-dir + configured writable roots}
      - command guard for known destructive/network-sensitive command classes
      - optional strict mode env:
          - CCR_DISABLE_NETWORK_COMMANDS=1 blocks known network binaries in Bash
  - not enforced in v1 lite:
      - kernel-level network isolation
      - syscall filtering
      - PID namespace isolation
      - mount namespace isolation

  ### Where checks occur

  1. SandboxPolicyAdapter pre-check in ToolExecutor
  2. PermissionEngine evaluation
  3. tool execution
  4. post-exec violation annotation in tool envelope

  ### Interaction with permission

  - sandbox hard denies override all permission modes including bypass
  - permission allow never bypasses hard path constraints
  - bypass skips prompts/rules only, not hard boundaries

  ### Workspace boundaries

  - default workspace root = --cwd
  - extra roots = repeated --add-dir
  - all paths resolve symlinks before boundary evaluation
  - path traversal (..) never bypasses canonical root checks

  ### v2 full sandbox deferred scope

  - process-level containment backend
  - outbound network policy enforcement
  - fine-grained FS allow/deny mount controls
  - sandbox violation telemetry stream

  ———

  ## SECTION 12 — MCP PLAN

  ### Why excluded from strict v1

  - v1 objective is stable local headless core.
  - MCP transport/auth/reconnect adds high integration risk and slows correctness of core priorities.

  ### Mandatory v1 abstractions (so v1.5 lands without re-architecture)

  - ExternalToolProvider interface in mcp/provider_interface.py
  - ToolRegistry accepts provider-sourced tool descriptors
  - ToolExecutor supports provider tag (local / external)
  - event schema includes optional provider field for tool events

  ### Exact v1.5 scope

  - stdio MCP transport only
  - startup tool discovery from configured servers
  - schema validation of MCP tool inputs
  - per-call timeout/cancel integration
  - MCP tool calls wrapped by same permission engine and transcript schema

  ### v2 MCP scope

  - SSE/HTTP/WebSocket transports
  - auth/session refresh/reconnect
  - resource and prompt-template APIs
  - tool/resource caching and policy-managed allowlists

  ———

  ## SECTION 13 — TEST STRATEGY

  ### Test layers

  - CLI contract tests
  - golden transcript conformance tests
  - permission determinism matrix tests
  - tool determinism tests
  - fault-injection tests
  - resume/recovery tests
  - schema compatibility tests

  ### Golden test inventory

  | Name | Purpose | Key assertions |
  |---|---|---|
  | cli_text_prompt_arg | text mode with positional prompt | exit 0, one assistant_message, no stream-only persistence |
  | cli_text_prompt_stdin | text mode stdin prompt | stdin consumed fully, final text emitted |
  | cli_json_output | json output contract | valid final JSON object shape |
  | cli_stream_json_basic | stream-json event flow | ordered NDJSON events, run terminal emitted |
  | cli_invalid_flag_combo | input/output mismatch | exit 2 with deterministic error message |
  | cli_allowed_disallowed_precedence | tool filtering precedence | disallowed tool blocked despite allow list |
  | cli_ask_unavailable_exit6 | ask mode no decision channel | denied with ask_unavailable, exit 6 |
  | stream_json_control_schema_validation | control message validation | unknown/invalid types rejected
  deterministically |
  | event_record_id_alignment | event-record identity | persisted record_id == event_id always |
  | event_parent_id_alignment | parent identity | persisted parent_id == parent_event_id always |
  | event_run_terminality_scope | run-scoped terminality | exactly one terminal event per run_id |
  | assistant_delta_stream_only | stream-only guarantee | no assistant_delta in JSONL |
  | event_tool_lifecycle_allow_order | tool allow ordering | requested -> decided -> started -> result -> finished |
  | event_tool_lifecycle_deny_order | tool deny ordering | requested -> decided(deny) -> result(denied) ->
  finished(denied) |
  | event_permission_required_only_when_askable | ask gating | no tool_permission_required when interactive unavailable
  |
  | permission_precedence_hard_boundary_first | precedence ordering | hard boundary deny beats allow/bypass |
  | permission_rule_conflict_deny_over_allow | effect conflict resolution | deny winner selected deterministically |
  | permission_rule_tiebreak_created_seq | tiebreak determinism | latest created_seq wins within same effect/source/
  specificity |
  | permission_once_consumption | once lifetime semantics | consumed only on winning decision |
  | permission_session_resume_replay | session rule recovery | session rules reconstructed from transcript |
  | permission_persistent_atomic_write | persistent durability | crash-safe rules write path |
  | permission_auto_read_allow | auto read flow | read tool allowed with auto_safe |
  | permission_auto_dangerous_ask | dangerous auto behavior | unresolved dangerous request requires ask |
  | permission_auto_dangerous_noninteractive | dangerous + no channel | deterministic deny ask_unavailable |
  | permission_bypass_with_hard_boundary | bypass does not skip hard deny | write outside workspace denied |
  | permission_decision_payload_required_fields | explanation completeness | all required fields present in
  permission_meta |
  | permission_audit_failure_fail_closed | audit fail-closed | audit append failure yields deny |
  | tool_read_truncation | large file read limits | byte/line truncation deterministic |
  | tool_ls_sorted | stable ls ordering | lexicographic output order |
  | tool_glob_sorted | stable glob ordering | sorted match list with cap |
  | tool_grep_ordering | grep deterministic ordering | ordered by path,line,column |
  | tool_edit_single_match | edit happy path | patch emitted, file updated, hash changed |
  | tool_edit_ambiguous_match_error | edit ambiguity | structured error AMBIGUOUS_MATCH |
  | tool_write_stale_token_error | stale write guard | structured error STALE_WRITE |
  | tool_bash_timeout | bash timeout semantics | timeout event + SIGTERM/SIGKILL path + timeout status |
  | retry_503_then_success | transient retry success | retry count and delay deterministic |
  | retry_retry_after_short | retry-after under threshold | exact delay honored |
  | fallback_on_529 | fallback model switch | fallback selected once per run |
  | fallback_exhaustion | fallback failure | run terminal PROVIDER_RETRY_EXHAUSTED |
  | interrupt_during_stream | model stream interrupt | interrupt event, no assistant_message from partial deltas |
  | interrupt_during_bash | tool interrupt propagation | running bash cancelled with result+finished |
  | interrupt_persistence_sequence | interrupted storage contract | interrupt -> notices -> run terminal order preserved
  |
  | resume_after_interrupt | interrupted run continuation | new run_id, session_resumed, stable state recovery |
  | resume_unfinished_tool_reconciliation | reconciliation rules | unfinished tool call closed deterministically |
  | continue_latest_session | continue index behavior | latest cwd session selected |
  | fork_session_resume | resume fork behavior | new session id, parent reference set |
  | transcript_tail_truncation_recovery | tail corruption handling | malformed final line truncated only |
  | transcript_schema_forward_compat | additive tolerance | unknown optional fields ignored in 1.x |
  | transcript_schema_major_break | major mismatch behavior | loader fails fast on 2.x |

  ### Additional requirements

  - every persisted line validates against transcript_record.schema.json.
  - every streamed event validates against events.schema.json.
  - fault-injection harness simulates provider/network/subprocess/transcript failures deterministically.
  - golden transcripts are snapshot-normalized for timestamps/IDs while preserving relative ordering invariants.

  ———

  ## SECTION 14 — IMPLEMENTATION PHASES

  ### Phase 0

  - Goal: lock contracts before implementation sprawl.
  - Deliverables:
      - event schema JSON
      - transcript schema JSON
      - tool result schema JSON
      - permission decision schema JSON
      - policy rules schema JSON
      - CLI flag contract doc + parser tests
  - Blocked-by: none
  - Done criteria:
      - schema validation tests passing
      - baseline CLI parsing tests passing

  ### Phase 1

  - Goal: runnable headless loop without tools.
  - Deliverables:
      - run_cli, parser, output/input modes
      - SessionOrchestrator, TurnStateMachine
      - model stream adapter + provider integration
      - event bus + stream-json emitter
  - Blocked-by: phase 0 schemas
  - Done criteria:
      - simple prompt round-trip works in text/json/stream-json
      - ordered event emission and persistence baseline passing

  ### Phase 2

  - Goal: local tool stack + deterministic permission engine.
  - Deliverables:
      - BaseTool, ToolRegistry, ToolExecutor
      - built-in tools: Read/LS/Glob/Grep/Bash/Edit/Write
      - PermissionEngine + RuleStore + audit log
      - sandbox lite path/command guards
  - Blocked-by: phase 1 runtime core
  - Done criteria:
      - permission determinism matrix tests passing
      - tool determinism tests passing
      - dangerous command auto/ask behavior verified

  ### Phase 3

  - Goal: durable transcript and resume correctness.
  - Deliverables:
      - TranscriptStore append-only + lockfile
      - SessionIndex for continue
      - ResumeLoader reconstruction logic
      - interrupted-run handling and reconciliation metadata
  - Blocked-by: phase 2 event completeness
  - Done criteria:
      - resume/continue/fork scenarios passing
      - transcript integrity and tail-recovery checks passing

  ### Phase 4

  - Goal: resilience behavior parity (retry/fallback/interrupt/timeout).
  - Deliverables:
      - RetryFallbackManager
      - timeout boundaries for model/tool/session
      - interrupt propagation end-to-end
      - partial-failure recovery path
  - Blocked-by: phase 2 tool runner + phase 3 persistence
  - Done criteria:
      - fault-injection suite green
      - fallback and retry-after scenarios green
      - interruption remains resumable with new run_id

  ### Phase 5

  - Goal: v1 hardening + v1.5 seams.
  - Deliverables:
      - performance tuning and log redaction
      - external tool provider interface (MCP placeholder)
      - final golden transcript corpus and docs
  - Blocked-by: phases 0-4 complete
  - Done criteria:
      - full golden inventory passes
      - stable CLI contract tagged v1.0

  ———

  ## SECTION 15 — EXPLICIT NON-GOALS

  - enterprise policy admin UI
  - distributed multi-host execution
  - agent-to-agent collaboration runtime
  - probabilistic policy decisions
  - automated policy tuning from telemetry
  - kernel-level sandbox isolation in v1
  - MCP transport/runtime in strict v1 execution path

  ———

  ## SECTION 16 — OPEN ASSUMPTIONS

  | Assumption | v1 resolution | What would upgrade it |
  |---|---|---|
  | Provider streaming quirks differ by vendor | normalize through ModelStreamAdapter and strict event contracts |
  provider-specific adapter conformance corpus |
  | Full sandbox-runtime behavior unavailable | v1 lite sandbox stays in-process policy | full sandbox backend spec and
  platform matrix |
  | External tool protocols still evolving | keep MCP behind v1.5 seam interfaces | finalized MCP transport/auth/
  reconnect contract |
  | Persistent rule storage may be externally edited | validate schema strictly; ignore invalid entries and emit notice
  | signed policy bundles or admin control plane |
  | Transcript files may end with crash-corrupted tail | deterministic malformed-tail truncation and notice |
  transactional append backend |

  ———

  ## SECTION 17 — FINAL IMPLEMENTATION SPEC SUMMARY

  - Exact system being built: a Python headless coding-agent runtime with deterministic event orchestration,
    deterministic permission policy evaluation, local tool execution, append-only transcripts, and reliable resume/
    recovery.
  - Guaranteed v1 properties:
      - run-scoped terminality with immutable prior runs
      - strict event-to-record mapping for persisted events
      - record_id == event_id and parent_id == parent_event_id
      - assistant_delta stream-only (never persisted)
      - deterministic permission precedence and conflict resolution
      - append-only transcript durability with deterministic recovery
  - Minimum end-to-end path:
      1. CLI parse and I/O mode validation
      2. session/run start event persistence
      3. model turn stream and tool orchestration
      4. permission engine decision and audit
      5. tool lifecycle events and result persistence
      6. run terminal event and resumable state handoff
  - Primary correctness gates:
      1. schema conformance (events, transcript, tool_result, permission_decision, policy_rules)
      2. permission precedence matrix
      3. deterministic lifecycle ordering
      4. interrupt/retry/fallback resilience
      5. resume reconstruction determinism

---

## Phase 1 boundary clarification

### Phase 1 boundary clarification

Phase 1 includes transcript durability. The Phase 1 runnable core must include append-only transcript persistence, seq allocation, parent linkage, assistant_delta as stream-only and never persisted, minimal single-writer safety, and session index update.

Phase 1 does not include full resume reconstruction. Resume reconstruction is deferred beyond Phase 1.

Concrete provider client implementation is deferred beyond Phase 1. Phase 1 uses provider abstraction plus fake/scripted provider behavior only. A provider_client seam/stub may exist only if required by the file tree, but no concrete network client is part of Phase 1.

Golden tests are authored in `docs/golden-tests.yaml`. Derived fixture inputs such as `fixtures/golden/golden_inventory_v1.yaml` must not become the authoritative source.

Phase 1 includes transcript durability. The Phase 1 runnable core must include:
- append-only transcript persistence
- seq allocation
- parent linkage
- assistant_delta as stream-only and never persisted
- minimal single-writer safety
- session index update

Phase 1 does not include full resume reconstruction. Resume reconstruction is deferred beyond Phase 1.

Concrete provider client implementation is deferred beyond Phase 1. Phase 1 uses provider abstraction plus fake/scripted provider only. A provider_client seam/stub may exist only if required by the file tree, but no concrete network client is part of Phase 1.

Golden tests are authored in `docs/golden-tests.yaml`. Any fixture YAML such as `fixtures/golden/golden_inventory_v1.yaml` is derived test input and must not become the authoritative source.
